::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which behavior best shows active listening in a discussion?
{
=A. Listening fully and paraphrasing before responding#Correct. Active listening means understanding first and responding thoughtfully.
~B. Interrupting as soon as you disagree#This prevents full understanding and weakens collaboration.
~C. Ignoring the speaker's main idea#This does not show listening.
~D. Changing the topic immediately#This avoids the speaker's message.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone best fits asking a Teacher of Record for help?
{
~A. Demanding and impatient#This tone may sound disrespectful and does not fit the audience.
~B. Sarcastic and vague#This can confuse or offend the listener.
=C. Respectful and specific#Correct. A help request should be clear, polite, and focused.
~D. Careless and rushed#This does not support clear communication.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence uses academic language clearly?
{
~A. The speech thing is good.#This is vague and does not identify a communication skill.
=B. The evidence supports the claim and helps the audience understand.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary.
~C. The stuff works well.#This does not name the idea clearly.
~D. It is okay for reasons.#This is unclear and unsupported.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best clarifies a school assignment?
{
~A. Just do the thing.#This is too vague to help.
~B. Guess what the teacher means.#Guessing does not support understanding.
~C. Write more so it looks done.#Length does not explain expectations.
=D. Do we need one paragraph or a list of bullet points?#Correct. This asks a specific question about the expected task.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "Our survey showed that 80 students want more practice time." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion because the speaker describes feelings#No feeling is described.
=B. Logic because the speaker uses data as evidence#Correct. Survey data supports the speaker's point with evidence.
~C. Trust because the speaker names a role#No role or credential is named.
~D. No rhetoric because numbers cannot persuade#Numbers can support a logical appeal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
What does it mean to paraphrase?
{
=A. Restate the idea in your own words without changing meaning#Correct. Paraphrasing keeps meaning while changing wording.
~B. Copy the speaker's exact words#Copying exactly is repeating, not paraphrasing.
~C. Add a new opinion before understanding#This skips the listening step.
~D. Change the idea to match your view#Changing meaning is not paraphrasing.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word is the most precise academic replacement for "stuff" when discussing support for a claim?
{
~A. things#This is still vague.
~B. whatever#This is informal and unclear.
~C. something#This remains vague.
=D. evidence#Correct. Evidence names information that supports a claim.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
A classmate says, "The audience may need a simpler example." Which response best builds on the idea?
{
~A. That idea is useless.#This dismisses the speaker.
~B. I was not listening.#This does not build on the idea.
=C. Good point; we could use one familiar school example before the evidence.#Correct. This connects to the idea and adds a useful next step.
~D. Stop talking about the audience.#This blocks discussion.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which sentence best matches a serious safety purpose?
{
=A. Please walk on the right side so everyone can move safely.#Correct. This is clear, respectful, and safety-focused.
~B. Whatever, just move around somehow.#This is careless and unclear.
~C. If you do not listen, that is your problem.#This is harsh and not helpful.
~D. Hallways are funny, so do anything.#This does not fit a serious safety purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "As a peer mentor, I have helped new students prepare for speeches." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Logic because the speaker gives data#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=B. Trust because the speaker gives relevant experience#Correct. Relevant experience builds credibility.
~C. Emotion because the speaker creates sadness#No emotion is emphasized.
~D. No appeal because it is short#Short statements can still build trust.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which feedback is most academically precise?
{
~A. The middle is weird.#This is vague and not useful.
~B. It needs better stuff.#This does not name the issue.
=C. The transition between reasons needs to be clearer.#Correct. This identifies the exact communication issue.
~D. It sounds kind of okay.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response gives clear instructional support?
{
~A. Do whatever sounds right.#This gives no clear direction.
~B. Make the answer longer.#Length is not the same as mastery.
~C. I am not explaining it.#This refuses support.
=D. First identify the audience, then explain why tone matters.#Correct. This gives a clear sequence.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response is a respectful disagreement?
{
~A. That is wrong and bad.#This is disrespectful and vague.
=B. I understand your idea, but I think a calmer tone fits the audience better.#Correct. It acknowledges the idea and gives a reason.
~C. My idea is the only good one.#This blocks collaboration.
~D. Stop sharing ideas.#This shuts down discussion.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Why does audience matter when choosing tone?
{
~A. Every audience needs the same tone.#Different audiences may need different tone.
~B. Tone should ignore the listener.#Tone affects how listeners receive a message.
~C. Audience matters only in essays.#Audience matters in speaking too.
=D. Tone should fit who is listening and why the message matters.#Correct. Audience and purpose guide tone.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "Imagine a classroom where everyone feels heard before decisions are made." Which appeal is strongest?
{
=A. Emotion because the speaker creates a feeling of belonging#Correct. The image helps the audience feel the value of being heard.
~B. Trust because a role is named#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. Logic because there is a statistic#No statistic appears.
~D. No appeal because it describes a classroom#The description is meant to affect the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
A speaker says, "The claim should come before the evidence." Which paraphrase is best?
{
~A. You think evidence should never appear.#This changes the meaning.
~B. You want only evidence and no claim.#This reverses the message.
=C. You are saying the main idea should be stated before the support.#Correct. This restates the meaning in new words.
~D. You do not care about order.#The speaker specifically discusses order.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which academic word names the people receiving the message?
{
~A. transition#A transition connects ideas.
~B. evidence#Evidence supports a claim.
=C. audience#Correct. Audience means the listeners or viewers receiving the message.
~D. whatever#This is not academic vocabulary.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best clarifies a speaker's meaning?
{
=A. When you say stronger opening, do you mean more serious or more emotional?#Correct. This asks a specific clarifying question.
~B. That makes no sense.#This states confusion without asking a useful question.
~C. I do not like openings.#This gives an opinion, not clarification.
~D. We should stop discussing it.#This ends the discussion.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence best explains a communication choice?
{
~A. It is good because it is good.#This repeats without explaining.
~B. The thing works.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. It sounds fine.#This is not specific enough.
=D. The opening question helps the audience focus on the main claim.#Correct. This explains the choice with precise vocabulary.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Which explanation best connects rhetoric to purpose?
{
~A. The speaker uses words because speeches need words.#This is too general.
=B. The speaker uses evidence to persuade the audience that the plan will work.#Correct. This names the choice and connects it to purpose.
~C. It sounds interesting.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~D. The speech has a topic.#This does not explain a rhetorical choice.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone is least appropriate for group feedback?
{
=A. Insulting and dismissive#Correct. Feedback should help improvement, not attack the speaker.
~B. Respectful and specific#This is appropriate.
~C. Calm and focused#This supports learning.
~D. Encouraging and clear#This helps collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which question best asks for clarification?
{
~A. This is impossible.#This expresses frustration but does not ask a specific question.
~B. Can someone do this for me?#This asks for completion, not clarification.
~C. Why is everything confusing?#This is too broad.
=D. Could you clarify whether the response should be spoken or written?#Correct. This names the unclear part.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best shows active listening?
{
~A. I disagree before hearing the full point.#This prevents understanding.
~B. I will ignore that idea.#This is not listening.
=C. You are saying the audience needs a clearer example first.#Correct. This restates the idea accurately.
~D. Your idea is boring.#This attacks the idea instead of listening.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "If each group uses a timer, speakers can keep their presentations within the limit." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Emotion because the speaker describes fear#No fear is described.
=B. Logic because the speaker explains cause and effect#Correct. Using a timer leads to staying within the limit.
~C. No rhetoric because timers are tools#The speaker uses reasoning about the tool.
~D. Trust because the speaker gives credentials#No credentials appear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence is the clearest academic feedback?
{
~A. The last part is there.#This gives no feedback.
~B. The end is nice.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. It finishes things.#This is imprecise.
=D. Your conclusion restates the claim and reminds the audience of the purpose.#Correct. It identifies the speech part and its function.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q26::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which phrase best describes voice in communication?
{
=A. The speaker's style or presence in the message#Correct. Voice includes how the speaker comes across.
~B. Only how loud a speaker talks#Voice is broader than volume.
~C. The number of words in the speech#Word count is not voice.
~D. The color of presentation slides#Slide color is not voice.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q27::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response helps a peer understand the next step?
{
~A. Just figure it out.#This is not helpful.
=B. First write your claim, then add one reason that supports it.#Correct. This gives clear instructional support.
~C. You missed the directions, so too bad.#This is disrespectful.
~D. Write random sentences.#This does not explain expectations.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q28::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.05
Question:
Which option is not a paraphrase?
{
~A. You think the evidence should come after the claim.#This can preserve the speaker's meaning.
~B. You are saying the audience needs a definition first.#This restates a likely message.
=C. You are wrong and should stop talking.#Correct. This is a judgment, not a paraphrase.
~D. You mean the tone should be more serious.#This can accurately restate meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q29::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Which appeal focuses on speaker credibility?
{
=A. Trust#Correct. Trust is connected to credibility and reliability.
~B. Emotion#Emotion focuses on feelings.
~C. Logic#Logic focuses on reasons and evidence.
~D. Confusion#Confusion is not a useful rhetorical appeal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q30::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best supports collaboration?
{
~A. I will decide for everyone.#This removes group participation.
~B. Nobody else should talk.#This blocks collaboration.
=C. Let's compare both ideas and choose the one that fits our audience.#Correct. This invites shared decision-making.
~D. Audience does not matter.#Audience matters in speech communication.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q31::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which tone best fits a speech encouraging students to join a cleanup project?
{
~A. Bitter and blaming#This may push the audience away.
~B. Confused and uncertain#This weakens the purpose.
~C. Sarcastic and dismissive#This does not invite participation.
=D. Encouraging and confident#Correct. This tone supports motivation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q32::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which revision replaces vague language with academic language?
{
~A. The thing should be better.#This remains vague.
=B. Add evidence after the claim so the audience understands your reason.#Correct. This uses precise academic language and explains the improvement.
~C. Make it sound right.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~D. It needs more stuff.#This does not name the need.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q33::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
Speech excerpt: "Every student deserves to feel welcome in discussion." Which appeal is strongest?
{
~A. Logic because the speaker gives a number#No number appears.
=B. Emotion because the speaker focuses on belonging#Correct. The statement appeals to fairness and feeling welcome.
~C. Trust because the speaker names a job#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~D. No appeal because it is about students#The statement is meant to affect how the audience feels.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q34::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.11
Question:
Which response best explains a submission expectation?
{
~A. Submit whatever.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~B. Do a thing online.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=C. Submit one paragraph that explains your audience, tone, and reason.#Correct. This clearly states what to submit.
~D. Make it look finished.#This does not explain the expectation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q35::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response shows listening before building?
{
=A. You are saying the audience needs an example; we could use a school scenario.#Correct. This paraphrases first and then builds.
~B. Your point does not matter.#This dismisses the speaker.
~C. I did not hear you, but I have an idea.#This skips listening.
~D. I will change the topic now.#This does not build on the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q36::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which vocabulary word means the reason for communicating?
{
~A. audience#Audience is who receives the message.
~B. evidence#Evidence supports a claim.
~C. transition#A transition connects ideas.
=D. purpose#Correct. Purpose is the reason for communicating.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q37::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.LST.06
Question:
What should a complete rhetorical explanation include?
{
~A. A personal opinion with no evidence#Evidence is needed.
~B. Only the word rhetoric#A label alone is not enough.
=C. The appeal, evidence from the excerpt, and how it supports purpose#Correct. This makes the explanation complete and text-based.
~D. Unrelated details#Details should connect to the rhetorical choice.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q38::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which message is most appropriate for a principal?
{
~A. Hey, I need this now.#This is too casual and demanding.
=B. Good morning. May I ask a question about the presentation schedule?#Correct. This is respectful, clear, and appropriate for the audience.
~C. Whatever, just answer me.#This is disrespectful.
~D. You should already know what I want.#This is unclear and rude.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q39::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence uses the clearest academic language?
{
~A. The speech part has stuff.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~B. The thing sounded nice.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~C. It was kind of okay.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=D. The introduction previews the main claim and prepares the audience.#Correct. This uses precise communication vocabulary.
}
::SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q40::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_Assessment_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which statement best summarizes Unit 1?
{
=A. Effective communication combines audience awareness, listening, tone, academic language, and rhetorical choices.#Correct. This summarizes the major Unit 1 skills.
~B. Audience does not affect communication.#Audience is central to Unit 1.
~C. Feedback should be vague.#Feedback should be precise and useful.
~D. Communication is only about speaking louder.#Communication includes listening, audience, tone, language, and rhetoric.
}
